---
/**
 * Hero — block 01. The page's only <h1>.
 *
 * ONE frame, both people in it. This replaced a split diptych — two 4:5
 * portraits divided by a 2px --color-brand-teal seam — which both the taste
 * audit and the brand assessment independently named as the page's loudest
 * contradiction: the logo's H is two figures JOINED at a crossbar, and the
 * hero took those same two figures and drew a wall between them. On a page
 * whose proposition is "a good match works both ways", the most prominent
 * graphic element must not mean separation. See C-22/C-23/C-24 in
 * docs/design/implementation-plan-2026-08-16.md.
 *
 * Do not re-split this visual. If a second image is ever wanted here, it
 * belongs somewhere else on the page.
 *
 * Imagery is a placeholder illustration, never photography of real people,
 * and it must never be captioned or described as an actual DirectHired
 * family, helper or staff member (master brief §55). The current file is an
 * AI-generated editorial illustration (DALL·E, prompt A1 in
 * docs/design/image-prompts-2026-08-16.md), supplied by DirectHired: two
 * adults of the same size facing each other over one bowl, a child at the
 * counter edge between them, one unbroken counter with a single thin teal
 * line along its front running the full width of the frame. Every figure is
 * FACELESS — no eyes, no mouths, no rendered expression — which is what keeps
 * it a drawing of a situation rather than a claim about people. Do not
 * replace it with anything that has faces short of a real, released
 * photograph. Swap the <Picture> source for the commissioned photograph when
 * it lands (shot brief: docs/design/brand-assessment-2026-08-15.md §7; this
 * slot is `hero-together` in the image registry, src/data/images.json) and
 * rewrite the alt text to describe that frame; nothing else here needs to
 * change, because width/height are fixed independently of the source file
 * and the aspect ratios are CSS.
 *
 * This image is the page's LCP element, and it is now a raster rather than
 * the 4KB vector it replaced — so its weight is a budget item, not a detail:
 *
 *   - <Picture> so the browser gets AVIF and falls back to WebP. The 2.2MB
 *     PNG master in src/assets/ is a build input and is never served; the
 *     largest thing that actually ships is the 1280w AVIF at ~29KB.
 *   - `widths` + `sizes` so a phone downloads a phone-sized file. `sizes`
 *     mirrors the four layout states below EXACTLY — full container width,
 *     the wider container padding at 48em, half the grid at 64em, and the
 *     bleed formula at 80em (50vw - 24px, derived in the 80em block). If any
 *     of those rules change, `sizes` has to change with them or the browser
 *     silently picks the wrong file.
 *   - 1280 is the top width on purpose: the widest real render is 1256 CSS px
 *     at a 2560px viewport, and the source is only 1402px across, so there is
 *     no 2x asset to be had. Asking for more would upscale, not sharpen.
 *
 * Keep loading="eager", fetchpriority="high" and the explicit width/height —
 * eager and fetchpriority because this is the LCP element (it regressed to
 * lazy once already), and the width/height pair plus the CSS aspect-ratio are
 * what hold CLS at 0.
 */
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets'
import Container from '../components/Container.astro'
import Button from '../components/Button.astro'
import { company } from '../data/company'
import heroTogether from '../assets/hero-together.png'
---

<section class="hero">
  <Container>
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-content">
        <h1>Find the Right Helper for Your Family</h1>
        <p class="hero-lede">
          Every family is different. We take the time to understand your needs before recommending a helper.
        </p>
        {/*
          One CTA only (D-12). On a phone the hero's buttons sit directly above
          the fixed bottom bar's two full-width buttons — four buttons, two
          labels, one screen, and none of them reading as the decision point.
          WhatsApp is not lost: it is permanently on screen in MobileCtaBar,
          and also in the mobile nav panel, pricing, final CTA and footer.
        */}
        <div class="hero-ctas">
          <Button variant="primary" href={company.requirementFormUrl}>Submit Your Requirements</Button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hero-visual">
        <Picture
          src={heroTogether}
          formats={['avif']}
          fallbackFormat="webp"
          widths={[440, 620, 780, 960, 1280]}
          sizes="(min-width: 80em) calc(50vw - 24px), (min-width: 64em) calc((min(100vw, 72rem) - 7rem) / 2), (min-width: 48em) calc(min(100vw, 72rem) - 4rem), calc(100vw - 3rem)"
          alt="Illustration of two adults at one kitchen counter, facing each other with their hands meeting over the same bowl, and a child reaching up to the counter between them"
          width={1280}
          height={1024}
          class="hero-image"
          loading="eager"
          decoding="async"
          fetchpriority="high"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </Container>
</section>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-block: var(--space-12) var(--space-section);
    /*
     * Contains the ≥80em bleed below. `clip`, not `hidden`: `hidden` would
     * make this a scroll container, and it would also force overflow-y to
     * auto. `clip` leaves the vertical axis visible and, crucially, means the
     * bleed can never produce horizontal document scroll — which is why the
     * bleed is expressed as a negative margin rather than a bare 100vw width
     * (100vw includes the scrollbar on Windows Chrome).
     */
    overflow-x: clip;
  }

  .hero-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-12);
    align-items: center;
  }

  .hero-content {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-6);
    max-width: 34rem;
  }

  .hero h1 {
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }

  .hero-lede {
    max-width: 42ch;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-lede);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .hero-ctas {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .hero-visual {
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    overflow: hidden;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-md);
  }

  /*
   * Nothing styles the <picture> that <Picture> wraps the <img> in, on
   * purpose: the reset in global.css gives `img, picture, svg, video`
   * display: block, and that is what stops an inline <picture> leaving a
   * text-baseline gap under the image — a hairline strip of page ground
   * inside the rounded, shadowed frame above. If that reset ever loses
   * `picture`, this frame is one of the places it will show.
   *
   * Landscape, because one room holding two people is a landscape problem.
   * 4:3 while the visual is full-container-width, 5:4 once it sits in a
   * half-width column (64em, the same breakpoint the grid splits at). The
   * source is drawn 5:4, so the mobile ratio is a ~3% crop top and bottom —
   * the illustration keeps everything meaningful clear of that band: the
   * counter line, both sets of hands and the bowl all sit near the vertical
   * centre, and object-fit: cover crops rather than squashes, so neither
   * ratio distorts the drawing.
   */
  .hero-image {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /* 64em is --bp-wide in tokens.css (named there, with this file listed as
     one of its three consumers alongside Process and TwoSidedMatch). Kept
     in sync by hand — custom properties do not resolve inside an @media
     condition. */
  @media (min-width: 64em) {
    .hero-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }

    .hero-image {
      aspect-ratio: 5 / 4;
    }
  }

  /*
   * 80em is --bp-xwide in tokens.css, which names this file and
   * Process.astro as its two consumers. Hand-synced, and note that THIS
   * FILE carries the literal twice: here, and in the first clause of the
   * <Picture> `sizes` attribute above. Change one and you must change the
   * other or the browser silently picks the wrong file.
   *
   * From here up the frame breaks the container's right edge and
   * runs to the viewport edge, giving the page its one asymmetric axis —
   * at 1920px every other element on the site obeys the same 1152px box with
   * 384px of dead margin each side.
   *
   * The margin resolves to (viewport − container) / 2 + the container's own
   * inline padding. 100vw counts the scrollbar, so this overshoots the true
   * edge by roughly half a scrollbar width; `overflow-x: clip` on .hero
   * absorbs that overshoot instead of opening a horizontal document scroll.
   * 72rem is Container's `default` max-width; --space-8 is its padding-inline
   * at ≥48em. Both are in Container.astro — keep in sync by hand.
   */
  @media (min-width: 80em) {
    .hero-visual {
      margin-inline-end: calc(((100vw - 72rem) / -2) - var(--space-8));
      border-start-end-radius: 0;
      border-end-end-radius: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
